<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS COMMENT SITS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT DELIBERATELY — a comment ABOVE the root
        breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with a misleading "unable to find matching parent
        xml file" error.

        WS3 (2026-08-30). This file is the FIELD only; the stamping logic lives in Apex.

        ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-08-30 (WS8 review, W5). This block used to end "This field has no
        writer of its own yet", written while a concurrent agent still owned that half. It
        landed the same day: LeadConvertService.buildProperty stamps this field at conversion
        (`p.Normalized_Address__c = ...`), PropertySelector.selectByNormalizedAddresses reads
        it, and scripts/backfill-property-normalized-address.apex fills pre-existing rows. The
        <description> below has said so since it was written — a reader who trusted this
        comment over that description would have concluded the reuse lookup was dead code and
        that nothing depended on the FLS grant this field needs.

        externalId=true, JUSTIFIED: WS3's whole purpose is a bulk reuse lookup — ONE
        PropertySelector.selectByNormalizedAddresses(Set<String>) call per LeadConvertService
        invocation, and that service fires TWICE in one transaction on a 251-conversion batch
        (LeadConvertServiceTest.bulkConversion251LeadsCreatesPropertiesAndStampsOpportunities
        drives 251 conversions across two convertLead chunks). Without an index this is an
        unindexed WHERE ... IN scan against a growing object, and Property__c is the root
        object of the acquisition graph — it gets Yardi/CoStar sync writes and every seed run,
        so its row count grows independently of conversions. Setting externalId=true is the
        only declarative way to get a custom index on a Text field without also forcing
        unique=true (a genuine Unique custom index requires unique=true, which is refused
        below). This does NOT create a duplicate-detection rule and does NOT block insert.

        unique=false, MANDATORY (not a default left alone): the org already contains
        duplicate Properties — reconciling that is WS3's whole point. A unique constraint
        would refuse the backfill script's UPDATE the moment two existing Property__c rows
        normalize to the same key, which is the exact scenario WS3 exists to find and reuse
        going forward. This mirrors Property_Registry__c.Normalized_Address__c (unique=false)
        and deliberately does NOT mirror Property_Registry__c.Property_Key__c (unique=true) -
        that field enforces a NEW claim key on a clean append-only registry; this field is
        being retrofitted onto an object with years of pre-existing duplicates.

        Length mirrors Property_Registry__c.Normalized_Address__c and Property_Key__c
        (255) deliberately: both hold the output of the SAME method,
        PropertyMatchingService.normalizeAddress, applied to the SAME upstream field length
        (*.Property_Address__c / Street, all Text(255)). normalizeAddress only removes and
        collapses characters, so its output can never exceed its input length - matching the
        length exactly rules out a truncation mismatch turning the same address into two
        different keys on the two objects.
    -->
    <fullName>Normalized_Address__c</fullName>
    <caseSensitive>false</caseSensitive>
    <externalId>true</externalId>
    <label>Normalized Address</label>
    <length>255</length>
    <required>false</required>
    <description>Lowercased, punctuation-stripped, whitespace-collapsed address key used to detect an existing Property for reuse on Lead conversion. The SOLE definition of the normalization is PropertyMatchingService.normalizeAddress - do not hand-derive this value anywhere else. Must resolve to the identical string Property_Registry__c.Property_Key__c would compute for the same address, so the two can never disagree. Populated by LeadConvertService at conversion (or by the WS3 backfill script for pre-existing rows); this field has no trigger of its own. Blank on any Property created outside LeadConvertService (hand-entry, seed scripts, future integrations) - such a Property is invisible to the reuse lookup and a duplicate may be created for it, which is bounded to today's existing behavior, not a new defect.</description>
    <inlineHelpText>Do not edit directly. Populated at Lead conversion from the normalized property address; used only to detect an existing Property for reuse.</inlineHelpText>
    <trackFeedHistory>false</trackFeedHistory>
    <type>Text</type>
    <unique>false</unique>
</CustomField>
